HOLyHammer
==========

HOLyHammer selects premises for a goal, translates the goal and premises to
TPTP, runs external provers on them in parallel time slices, and replays a
proof they found with Metis through the HOL4 kernel.  The provers are never
trusted, and a hammer proof adds no oracle tag.

`examples/README.md` is the user guide: worked examples, tips, and a FAQ.
This file covers the options, the schedule, and the installation of the
provers.

Usage
-----

At least one external prover must be installed (see "Installing provers").

    load "holyHammer"; open holyHammer;
    holyhammer ``1 + 1 = 2``;
    g `1 + 1 = 2`; e hh;

`holyhammer : term -> thm` proves a closed statement and `hh` is the same
search as a tactic, assumptions included.  `hh_pb dir provers premises` runs
the given provers on exactly the given premises (fully qualified names, such
as `"listTheory.EVERY_MEM"`), writing its problems below `dir`.  Each returns
the first proof Metis replays and prints the `metis_tac` call to put in a
script.

Options
-------

    hhConfig.hh_set ("timeout", "60");
    hhConfig.hh_set ("provers", "e vampire");
    hhConfig.hh_get "timeout";
    hhConfig.hh_unset "timeout";
    hhConfig.print_params ();

`hh_set` validates the value at once.  `print_params` prints every option
with its value, where the value comes from, and a short description.  An
option takes its value from, in order: `hh_set`; the user configuration
`$HOME/.hol4/hammer/config`; the system configuration
`$HOLDIR/etc/hammer-config`; the environment variable `HOL4_HAMMER_<NAME>`
(the option name in upper case, such as `HOL4_HAMMER_TIMEOUT`); the default.
The configuration files hold one `name = value` per line.

The options are `timeout`, `provers`, `slices`, `cores`, `export_workers`,
`filter`, `max_facts`, `format`, `type_enc`, `lam_trans`, `mono_iters`,
`mono_instances`, `minimize`, `preplay_timeout`, `minimize_timeout`,
`preplay_drop_one`, `cache`, `cache_dir`, `cache_max_entries`,
`debug_dir`, and `verbose`.  `set_timeout n` is a deprecated alias for
`hh_set ("timeout", Int.toString n)`.

By default a call prints only its outcome: the verified tactic or why it
failed.  With `verbose` it also reports every slice as it starts and ends.

Each call writes its TPTP problems to a fresh directory below the state
directory (`$HOME/.hol4/hammer`, or `$HOL4_HAMMER_DIR`) and deletes it when
the call returns.  With `debug_dir` set, the problems are kept in
`debug_dir/problems`, next to the raw prover output.

Time limits
-----------

`timeout` (30 s by default) is the wall-clock budget of the proof search,
counted from the launch of the provers.  At the timeout no further slice
starts, and the provers still running are stopped after a short grace
period.  The proofs the provers found are still reconstructed until one
replays, so a call can return after the timeout.

Each proof is replayed by Metis within `preplay_timeout` (2.5 s), and with
`minimize` set (the default) shortened by attempts of at most
`minimize_timeout` (1.0 s) each.  With `preplay_drop_one` (the default) a
proof that fails to replay is retried once without each of its lemmas in
turn.  These limits are CPU seconds of the replaying thread, so a replay
beside busy provers is not cut short; outside Linux they are the lesser of
wall time and the process's CPU time.

`cores` (0, the default, means all processors) is the number of provers
run at once, and `export_workers` (default 2) the number of threads that
write the problems.

Schedule
--------

The default schedule has 24 slices, each with its own prover, premise
filter, number of facts, TPTP format, type encoding, and lambda
translation.  The first 16 slices select premises by kNN; the last eight
use MePo, MaSh, and MeSh.  The slices use FOF and typed `tx0`, `tx0-`,
`th0`, and `th1` problems.  `slices` caps the number of slices (0, the
default, runs them all) and `max_facts` the facts per slice.

`filter`, `format`, `type_enc`, and `lam_trans` override the choice of every
slice; empty, the default, keeps each slice's own.

  * Filters: `knn` (dependency-aware k-nearest neighbours), `mepo`
    (relevance by the goal's constants and types), `mash` (sparse Naive
    Bayes meshed with kNN), `mesh` (MePo and MaSh combined), and `none`
    (all available premises in dependency order).
  * Formats: `fof`, `tf0`, `tf1`, `tx0`, `tx0-`, `th0`, and `th1`.
  * Type encodings: `mono_native`, `mono_native_fool`, `mono_native_higher`,
    `mono_native_higher_fool`, `poly_native`, `mono_guards`, and
    `mono_guards??`; empty selects the untyped FOF translation.
  * Lambda translations: `lifting`, `combs`, `combs_and_lifting`, and
    `keep_lams`.

A combination the slice's prover does not support is rejected before the
search.  `mono_iters` sets the monomorphisation rounds and `mono_instances`
caps the new instances (empty, the default, uses the slice's fact count, at
least the prover's own cap).

The provers are the constructors of `holyHammer.prover`: `Eprover`,
`Vampire`, `Zipperposition`, and `Z3`, named `e`, `vampire`,
`zipperposition`, and `z3` in the `provers` option.  Z3 has no slices, so
scheduling it alone runs nothing.

Installing provers
------------------

    tools/download-provers

installs the tested versions:

  * E 3.2.5-ho;
  * Vampire 5.0.1; and
  * Zipperposition 2.1 (Isabelle component revision 2).

It installs each prover in `<prover>-<version>/<platform>/`, where
`<platform>` is such as `x86_64-linux` or `arm64-darwin`, below the first
of: its `--dest` argument, `$HOL4_HAMMER_DIR/provers`, and
`$HOME/.hol4/hammer/provers`.  Alternatively, put a supported binary on
`PATH`: `eprover-ho` or `eprover`, `vampire`, `zipperposition`, or
`z3_tptp`, Z3's TPTP front end built from `examples/tptp` in the Z3 sources
(tested with 4.11.2).  The plain `z3` binary does not read TPTP and is never
used.

A prover is found by the first of:

  1. the configuration entry `prover.<name>.executable`, for example
     `prover.e.executable = /opt/e/bin/eprover`;
  2. its environment variable: `HOL4_EPROVER_EXECUTABLE`,
     `HOL4_VAMPIRE_EXECUTABLE`, `HOL4_ZIPPERPOSITION_EXECUTABLE`, or
     `HOL4_Z3_TPTP_EXECUTABLE` (HolSmt's `HOL4_Z3_EXECUTABLE` names an
     SMT-LIB solver and is not consulted);
  3. `PATH`;
  4. an installation in the state directory's `provers`, then in
     `$HOME/.hol4/hammer/provers`: the newest version directory holding
     the binary for this platform.

To check the installed provers end to end:

    cd src/holyhammer
    ../../bin/Holmake smoke.exe && ./smoke.exe
